from review import card,save,DATE

def b(title,text,citation=None):
 d=dict(title=title,text=text)
 if citation:d['citation']=citation
 return d

def sm(sentences,refs):return dict(sentences=sentences,basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=refs)
def imp(c,reason):return dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason=reason,assessed_on=DATE)

id='TCS-1882';_,c=card(id);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='TQC 2025, article 7: Conjecture 2, Theorem 1, Table 1 and Sections 3.1–3.2 read. The main target uses arbitrary synthesizer access; the string-oracle lower bound is a stronger conjecture.'
refs.append(dict(id='preprint',title='Uniformity testing when you have the source code — version record',authors="Clément L. Canonne, Robin Kothari, Ryan O’Donnell",year=2024,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04972v1',locator='Version 1, 7 November 2024. Abstract and revision history checked on 12 September 2026; the joint parameter lower bound remains conjectural in this record.'))
save(id,dict(title='Tight quantum query lower bounds for uniformity testing',formal='Does testing whether an unknown distribution p on [d] is exactly uniform or has total variation distance greater than ε from uniform require Ω(min{d^{1/3}/ε^{4/3}, √d/ε}) quantum queries to a synthesizer for p, with success probability at least 0.99 on every promised input? The bound is uniform in d≥2 and 0<ε≤1/4; this explicit small-error range avoids the empty or near-maximal-distance promises implicit in an unrestricted reading of the source.',definitions='Write [d]={1,…,d}, u_d(i)=1/d and d_TV(p,u_d)=(1/2)Σ_i|p_i−1/d|. The algorithm receives d and ε and oracle access to a unitary U_p satisfying U_p|0^k⟩=Σ_i√p_i|i⟩|ψ_i⟩, where each garbage state |ψ_i⟩ is normalized. The extension of U_p away from |0^k⟩ and its garbage states are arbitrary; the tester must work for every valid oracle satisfying the promise. A query is one application of U_p, U_p† or controlled-U_p; intervening quantum operations, ancillas and measurements are unrestricted and do not count toward query complexity. The code is used as a black box: the tester does not get a classical description to inspect for free. Let Q(d,ε) be the minimum worst-case number of such queries among testers whose probability of the correct yes/no output is at least 0.99 for every oracle with p=u_d or d_TV(p,u_d)>ε, taking the worst case over allowed oracle-register sizes. No guarantee is required in the intermediate gap. The conjecture asks for an absolute c>0 with Q(d,ε)≥c min{d^{1/3}ε^{-4/3},d^{1/2}ε^{-1}} throughout the stated parameter range, up to conventional finite asymptotic exceptions.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Prove the uniform two-parameter lower bound in this oracle model, or refute it by a tester with a smaller worst-case asymptotic query complexity in a valid parameter regime. Lower bounds for classical samples, one particular algorithm, constant ε only, or a restricted family of quantum testers do not establish the full claim.',context_blocks=[b('Running a sampler backwards is extra information','Ordinary sample access reveals only independent outcomes. Coherent access to a sampler and its inverse permits interference between executions. This can reduce the number of calls needed to detect a departure from uniformity; a classical sample lower bound therefore does not automatically apply.'),b('The upper bound has two regimes','The paper establishes O(min{d^{1/3}/ε^{4/3},√d/ε}) queries. The terms cross at ε=d^{-1/2}: the first governs larger errors and the second smaller errors. The question asks whether any quantum tester can improve this full dependence.', 'primary'),b('Separate lower bounds leave a gap','The source recalls Ω(d^{1/3}) queries for constant ε and Ω(1/ε) already on a two-element domain. Knowing both separately does not yield their conjectured joint dependence. For example, at ε=d^{-1/2} the target is Ω(d).', 'primary'),b('A stronger variant fixes a special oracle structure','For a string x∈[d]^m, the standard oracle maps |j,z⟩ to |j,z⊕x_j⟩ and defines p_i=|{j:x_j=i}|/m. The authors also conjecture the same lower bound even with this structure available, in the worst case over m and x. This is a stronger claim than the arbitrary-synthesizer target selected here; fixed small m can cap the cost through reading the entire string.', 'primary')],why='Would identify the optimal joint dependence on domain size and accuracy for one of the basic distribution-testing tasks with coherent sampler access, clarifying the extent of quantum advantage over classical samples.',progress=[dict(date='2025',text='The TQC paper supplies the matching candidate upper bound and states its optimality as Conjecture 2.',citation='primary')],references=refs,status='source_open',status_note='Conjecture, access definitions, version record and later-result searches checked through 12 September 2026. No matching general quantum-query lower bound was located; classical uniformity and quantum-state measurement results use different access models.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=imp(c,'A fundamental quantum-query optimality question for distribution testing; the existing numerical importance score is retained.'),review_note='Recovered both exponents, success probability and oracle access. Selected the principal synthesizer conjecture and identified the stronger string-oracle variant separately. Explicitly fixed the usual small-error range rather than asserting a false bound for an empty promise at ε≥1−1/d.',working_summary=sm(['A quantum tester must distinguish exact uniformity from a distribution whose total variation distance exceeds ε.','It can invoke a coherent sampler, its inverse and a controlled invocation.','The best known query upper bound is the smaller of d^{1/3}/ε^{4/3} and √d/ε.','The question is whether every tester must pay this cost in the worst case across both parameters.','The source also proposes a stronger lower bound for string oracles, whose extra structure is kept separate from the main target.'],['primary','preprint'])),notes=['Recovered the exact two-parameter lower bound and synthesizer query model.','Specified worst-case success, promise gap and an explicit nondegenerate small-error range.','Separated the stronger string-oracle variant and the known one-parameter lower bounds.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29809'])

id='TCS-1905';_,c=card(id);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='MFCS 2025, article 40: Definitions 1, 3 and 4, Section 3.2 and final paragraph of Section 6 read. The paper defines symmetric IPS, but explicitly leaves the appropriate symmetric VP/VNP analogues for the proposed implication to be developed.'
refs.append(dict(id='preprint',title='Symmetric Proofs in the Ideal Proof System — version record',authors='Anuj Dawar, Erich Grädel, Leon Kullmann, Benedikt Pago',year=2025,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.16820v1',locator='Version 1, 23 April 2025. Full abstract and revision history checked on 12 September 2026.'))
issue='The source does not define the proposed symmetric analogues of VP and VNP. A complete target must fix the base field, variable and group-action families, size/degree conventions, and how Boolean summation and any relevant reductions respect those actions. The source also leaves the corresponding CNF-family and symmetry quantifiers implicit. Merely assigning names to classes chosen to make the implication true would not recover its intended content.'
save(id,dict(title='Do symmetric IPS lower bounds imply a symmetric VP–VNP separation?',formal='For suitably defined symmetric analogues of VP and VNP, would superpolynomial lower bounds on symmetric Ideal Proof System refutations of unsatisfiable Boolean CNF formulas imply that symmetric VP differs from symmetric VNP? The source poses the development of those analogue classes as part of the question; they have not been specified here.',definitions='A Boolean CNF is a conjunction of clauses, each a disjunction of literals. Over a fixed field K, encode each clause by the product of the factors that equal one when its literals are false, set that product to zero, and add x_i²−x_i=0 for Boolean variables. For example, x∨¬y becomes (1−x)y=0. Let f_1(x),…,f_m(x) be the resulting polynomials. An IPS certificate is a polynomial C(x,y) with C(x,0)=0 and C(x,f_1(x),…,f_m(x))=1 as polynomial identities; an IPS refutation is an arithmetic circuit over K computing C. A group Γ acts by permutations of the x variables and preserves the polynomial axiom set; it then permutes y_i according to the induced permutation of f_i. The circuit is Γ-symmetric when each group permutation extends to a permutation of its gates preserving wires, operation labels and field constants. Polynomial invariance of its output alone is weaker than this circuit requirement. Size is measured by circuit gates with the instance-size floor; a superpolynomial family lower bound excludes any one polynomial bound in encoded formula size. In the ordinary nonsymmetric setting, VP consists of polynomial-degree polynomial families in polynomially many variables having polynomial-size arithmetic circuits. VNP consists of families expressible as a sum over polynomially many Boolean witness variables of such a VP family. How to impose compatible symmetries on these families and on the summation is a missing part of the proposed analogue.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Specify and justify the symmetric algebraic classes and compatible CNF/group families, then prove or refute the stated implication under those fixed definitions. A lower bound for invariant polynomials, for a bounded-degree IPS fragment, or only for Hilbert-like certificates does not automatically establish a lower bound for the full symmetric IPS system.',context_blocks=[b('A short algebraic identity certifies impossibility','If all axioms f_i vanished at a common Boolean assignment, substituting them into a valid certificate would give both C(x,0)=0 and C(x,f(x))=1. The certificate rules out that assignment by a polynomial identity, with its arithmetic circuit providing a compact representation.'),b('Symmetry is a restriction on the proof itself','Relabeling an instance must extend through the entire circuit. Two circuits computing the same invariant polynomial can differ in whether such gate permutations exist. This makes symmetric proof complexity a distinct question from ordinary circuit complexity.', 'primary'),b('The ordinary implication motivates the question','The source recalls the Grochow–Pitassi connection: superpolynomial unrestricted IPS lower bounds for Boolean CNFs imply VP≠VNP. Restricting proofs to symmetric circuits does not by itself establish the analogous conclusion for newly defined symmetric classes.', 'primary'),b('Known fragment separations do not settle the full target','The paper compares symmetric proof size, degree and linearity, and obtains nontrivial upper bounds for several standard formula families. A separation between those proof fragments is not yet a separation of the symmetric algebraic classes asked for in the final question.', 'primary')],why='Would connect structural restrictions on algebraic proofs with a corresponding arithmetic complexity separation, giving a precise algebraic interpretation of the power lost by requiring proofs to respect symmetries.',progress=[dict(date='2025',text='The MFCS paper defines symmetric IPS and poses the VP/VNP analogue question after its proof-size, degree and linearity results.',citation='primary')],references=refs,status='uncertain',status_note='Source definitions, final question, version record and subsequent searches checked through 12 September 2026. The necessary symmetric algebraic classes remain unspecified in the source; no complete matching formulation and resolution was located.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(reviewed_on=DATE,status='needs_specification',notes=['Recovered the defined symmetric proof system and separated it from the undeveloped algebraic classes.'],remaining_issue=issue),importance=imp(c,'A structural connection between algebraic proof complexity and symmetric arithmetic circuit classes; existing numerical score retained.'),review_note='Replaced the dangling sentence with its complete implication, expanded IPS identities and structural symmetry, and exposed the missing class definitions. Used the source prose’s instance-size floor for proof size: its displayed min conflicts with the immediately following “at least” explanation, so that typographical minimum is not reproduced.',working_summary=sm(['An IPS refutation encodes unsatisfiability as a polynomial identity computed by an arithmetic circuit.','A symmetric refutation must let every permitted instance relabeling extend to the circuit gates.','The source asks whether proving such refutations require superpolynomial size would separate symmetric analogues of VP and VNP.','The ordinary IPS-to-algebraic-complexity connection motivates this proposed implication.','The appropriate symmetric algebraic classes and their compatibility with the formula families still need to be defined.'],['primary','preprint'])),state='improved_unfinished',notes=['Recovered the complete proposed symmetric VP/VNP implication.','Defined IPS certificates and gate-level symmetry with the induced axiom action.','Recorded the missing class definitions and separated full IPS from restricted fragments.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
